  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Society Name Board",
    "overview": "RentRoll is the painted resident board from a building's entrance, made live. A bottle-green enamel shell holds white plates, each with a brass room number, and the board reads at a glance because every plate's form says where that room's rent stands.\n\nThe system is dense but calm, built for a phone held in one hand in bright daylight. One typeface carries everything, money figures are the loudest type, and color stays scarce enough that a red plate means something the moment it appears. Plates sit flat like painted metal and only respond when touched.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Bottle-green shell, enamel-white plates, brass number tags.",
      "Status told by plate form and words, with color as reinforcement.",
      "Hind at every level, with tabular rupee figures.",
      "Flat at rest, with depth reserved for touch and floating confirmation.",
      "Bottom tabs on phones, a green side rail from 900px."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Red Means Late Rule",
        "body": "Signal Red marks overdue rent and nothing else. Errors and destructive actions use Board Ink with a heavy edge, so red never loses its meaning.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Brass Is Never Text On White Rule",
        "body": "Brass carries ink on top of it, or sits on green. It never sets words on a light surface.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Money Rule",
        "body": "Every rupee figure and room number uses tabular numerals so amounts compare cleanly down a column.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Family Rule",
        "body": "Hind carries all interface type. Email alone falls back to Arial, because mail clients cannot load it.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Painted Metal Rule",
